-- title:
--   Residue of the local Hecke algebra structure map equals θ
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer $N \ge 1$ and a set $S$ of natural numbers, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, complete with respect to the adic filtration of its maximal ideal. Let $\mathbb{T} =$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) be the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of the space of weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators $U_q$ for primes $q \mid N$ with $q \notin S$, and let $\theta : \mathbb{T} \to k(\mathcal{O})$ be a ring homomorphism to the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$. Assume [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N 2`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6), i.e. that the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule spanned by the weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers is the whole space. Write $\mathbb{T}_\theta =$ [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) for the localisation of `heckeBaseAlgebra N S 𝒪` at the submonoid obtained by transporting, along `latticeBaseChange N S 𝒪`, the complement of the prime ideal `heckeCharKernel N S 𝒪 θ`, and let [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151) be its structure map from $\mathbb{T}$. Then for every $t \in \mathbb{T}$ the residue of $\pi(t)$ in the residue field of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ coincides with the image of $\theta(t)$ under the map of residue fields induced by the structure morphism $\mathcal{O} \to \mathbb{T}_\theta$. Nothing is asserted about injectivity of $\pi$ or about any further structure of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$.
--
--   This is the defining compatibility of the local Hecke algebra attached to a residual eigensystem $\theta$: the localised Hecke algebra is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra whose residue field receives $k(\mathcal{O})$, and the Hecke operators reduce there to their prescribed $\theta$-eigenvalues. It is used wherever the residual behaviour of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ must be identified with $\theta$, for instance in the construction of the Galois representation datum attached to $\theta$ and in the flatness and ordinarity statements at individual primes.
